---
title: Smoky Roasted Salsa Roja
description: With guidance from Noah TheFiestaDog himself.
image: https://www.kitchengidget.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/03/Guajillo-Chile-Salsa-2a.jpg
servings: 6
cuisine: Mexican
---

Bring a #medium pot{} of @water{} to a boil.

Add @ancho chiles{2}(dried, stems removed, deseeded) and @guajillo chiles{2}(dried, stems removed, deseeded) and reduce heat to a simmer. Cook/rehydrate for around ~{25%minutes} or until softened. Be sure to keep the chili water afterwards.

Place @roma tomatoes{4.5}(top and bottom removed only), @onion{2/3}(white, just the middle rings), @jalapeño peppers{2}(stems removed, deseeded), @serrano peppers{2}(stems removed, deseeded), @habaneros{2}(stems removed, deseeded. If you like heat, add up to 6 total), rehydrated chiles, and @garlic{4%cloves}(whole) in a #cast iron pan{}. Drizzle with @olive oil{}.

Broil on the top-most shelf of your oven for ~{5-10%minutes}, or just until the ingredients start to char.

In a #food processor{} (or blender), combine your ingredients with @?brown sugar{2%tsp}, @?lime{1}(juiced), and @?mango{1}(medium, peeled and chopped), about ~{1%minute}. If you prefer chunky salsa, leave some ingredients out until the last few seconds of blending. If you find the salsa to be too thick, add the chili water from earlier.

> If you find this still isn't spicy enough for you, replace the guajillo with chile de arbol.

> I've been told you can follow pretty much any recipe, but don't trust anything the uses oregano. Some sample recipes are: https://www.kitchengidget.com/2016/03/19/guajillo-chile-salsa-recipe/ and https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MUYRIgzSBsI&t=34s 